ntrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). Review feedback is added by the review-feedback loop. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n22\t 5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n23\t-6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `<type>: <issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and is `chore` otherwise. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n24\t+6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n25\t 7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the host (kept 7 days) and are never committed.\n26\t 8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are the role's `reviewers`, or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty (`when:` rules are ignored until #42), plus any `review:<role>` the lead deferred, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\n27\t 9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR.\n28\tdiff --git a/internal/committer/code.go b/internal/committer/code.go\n29\tindex 7ab8d8d..4ec8829 100644\n30\t--- a/internal/committer/code.go\n31\t+++ b/internal/committer/code.go\n32\t@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@ type CodeRequest struct {\n33\t \tIssue  int        // > 0 (dry-run: < 0 allowed, synthetic result, as Request)\n34\t \tRole   string     // ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$\n35\t \tJobID  string     // required: the Ard-Job trailer\n36\t-\tType   string     // ^[a-z]{1,16}$, e.g. feat, fix, chore\n37\t+\tType   string     // ^[a-z]{1,16}$, e.g. feat, fix; empty means no prefix\n38\t \tTitle  string     // untrusted issue title\n39\t \tBody   string     // untrusted summary, already redacted by the caller\n40\t \tBase   string     // 40-hex SHA the job's worktree started from\n41\t@@ -51,7 +51,7 @@ var (\n42\t \n43\t // CodeMessage builds:\n44\t //\n45\t-//\t<type>: <title> (#<issue>)\n46\t+//\t[<type>: ]<title> (#<issue>)\n47\t //\n48\t //\t<body>\n49\t //\n50\t@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@ var (\n51\t // No [skip ci]: PR-branch commits always run CI.\n52\t func CodeMessage(typ, title string, issue int, body, jobID string) (string, error) {\n53\t \tswitch {\n54\t-\tcase !typeRe.MatchString(typ):\n55\t+\tcase typ != \"\" && !typeRe.MatchString(typ):\n56\t \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"%w: bad type %q\", ErrInvalid, typ)\n57\t \tcase !jobRe.MatchString(jobID):\n58\t \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"%w: bad or missing job id\", ErrInvalid)\n59\t@@ -83,7 +83,10 @@ func CodeMessage(typ, title string, issue int, body, jobID string) (string, erro\n60\t \tif t == \"\" {\n61\t \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"%w: empty title\", ErrInvalid)\n62\t \t}\n63\t-\tprefix, suffix := typ+\": \", fmt.Sprintf(\" (#%d)\", issue)\n64\t+\tprefix, suffix := \"\", fmt.Sprint